course: dictionary
lesson: 740
title: A word with a history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bastille was a fortress prison in Paris, and you may meet the name in history or on a French day out. «It remembers 14 July 1789, when people in Paris took the Bastille, a prison fortress.» The clues are fortress and prison: it was a strong building where people were locked up.
explain: This word comes from French, so it may look strange at first. Ask: what would fit here? A fort or a prison fits the sentence, so the clues help you work out what it means.
explain: Context also tells you when a word is a name for a place or a day. «France's national day is 14 July, le 14 Juillet or la fête nationale; English speakers often call it Bastille Day.» The words national day and the date show that Bastille Day is a special day for the whole country, not a building.
fact: A bastille is a fortress or castle, from the French word.
fact: «Bastille» means a fortress prison in Paris, which crowds stormed on 14 July 1789.
fact: The storming of the Bastille was the start of the French Revolution.
fact: «Bastille Day» is 14 July, France's national day, also called la fête nationale.
fact: A capital letter and a date can be clues that a word is a name, not an ordinary word.
exercise: «It remembers 14 July 1789, when people in Paris took the Bastille, a prison fortress.» What was the Bastille? | A French palace full of gardens | A kind of firework | A parade of soldiers | *A fortress prison in Paris
exercise: In French, bastille means what? | A day of parades | A river in Paris | A bird that sings at night | *A fort or castle
exercise: What did the storming of the Bastille start? | *The French Revolution | A new bridge over the river | A school holiday | A football match
test: «France's national day is 14 July, le 14 Juillet or la fête nationale; English speakers often call it Bastille Day.» What do English speakers often call 14 July? | Revolution Week | *Bastille Day | Fireworks Night | Paris Holiday
test: In the sentence about Bastille Day, which word tells you it is a country's special day? | Bastille | often | English | *national
exam: «...a parade in Paris in the morning, fireworks at night and dances at the fire station.» In this sentence, what happens at night? | Prison visits | Lessons | Parades | *Fireworks
